      "id": 1,
      "text": "White Oak got its name from two white oak trees that stood beside the first school built near Hawkins Creek.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        28,
        29
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/White_Oak%2C_Texas"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "In the late 1800s, White Oak was a small farming community centered around three sawmills on thick forested land.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        19,
        20,
        21
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/White_Oak%2C_Texas"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The original school, erected near Hawkins Creek, doubled as a church on Sundays for the early settlers.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        22
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/White_Oak%2C_Texas"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "After the 1885 arson that destroyed the first school, a new building opened in 1887 on donated land by Tuttle and Bumpus.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        23,
        24,
        26,
        27
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/White_Oak%2C_Texas"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "White Oak was officially incorporated as a city in 1960, formalizing its local government structure.",
